ds for all 2-connected graphs, then it holds for all graphs G with δ(G) ≥ 2. Recently, Lu at al. [53] addressed Conjecture 2 and showed that dim(G) ≤ 2c(G) + 1 when G has minimum degree at least 2.\n\n**Conjecture 3 ([64]).** Let G be a connected graph. If G ≠ C<sub>n</sub>, then mdim(G) ≤ ℓ(G) + 2c(G).\n\nConjecture 3 is known to hold for trees [42], cacti and 3-connected graphs [64], and balanced theta graphs [63]. The following conjecture on distance-edge-monitoring sets was also posed recently.\n\n**Conjecture 4 ([30,31]).** For any graph G, dem(G) ≤ c(G) + 1.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSedlar–Škrekovski mixed metric-dimension conjecture | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/383319/sedlar-skrekovski-mixed-metric-dimension-conjecture)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # Sedlar–Škrekovski mixed metric-dimension conjecture\n\nFor every connected graph $G$G that is not a cycle, the mixed metric dimension satisfies $\\operatorname{mdim}(G)\\leq \\ell(G)+2c(G)$mdim(G)≤ℓ(G)+2 c(G), where $\\ell(G)$ℓ(G) is the number of leaves and $c(G)$c(G) is the cyclomatic number.\n\n# Sedlar–Škrekovski mixed metric-dimension conjecture\n\nFor every connected graph $G$G that is not a cycle, the mixed metric dimension satisfies $\\operatorname{mdim}(G)\\leq \\ell(G)+2c(G)$mdim(G)≤ℓ(G)+2 c(G), where $\\ell(G)$ℓ(G) is the number of leaves and $c(G)$c(G) is the cyclomatic number. Moreover, equality holds if and only if $G$G is either a cactus graph in which every cycle has precisely one vertex of degree at least $3$3, or a balanced $\\Theta$Θ-graph.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\narXiv\n\n## Additional references\n\n  * Graphs attaining an upper bound on the mixed metric dimension — arXiv\n\nPosted 1 month ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Refreshed 1 month ago\n\nClaimed solved\n\nA new unrefereed preprint claims the conjecture’s equality cases are completely classified, but independent confirmation is not 